odule is blocked from loading immediately.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-55de29ecc7a0494b\",\"cmd-4b731f48756449b2\",\"cmd-2f8d02ea92144dc2\",\"cmd-caa57d6719044407\"]},{\"id\":\"persistent-block\",\"requirement\":\"usb-storage kernel module is prevented from loading after a reboot\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A blacklist/install-false modprobe.d config was written and initramfs updated (cmd-4b731f48756449b2). A real reboot was performed (cmd-71cc473d5cdf45e8), boot_id changed from 901ef286... to d397e892... (cmd-e44f6c1f1b76433f, cmd-fe5e3ecd4c404e05), and post-reboot verification (cmd-1a9afa498dc44815) confirmed the config file was intact and modprobe usb-storage still failed (rc=1) with the module unloaded — a direct before/disruption/after demonstration of the persistence requirement.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4b731f48756449b2\",\"cmd-e44f6c1f1b76433f\",\"cmd-71cc473d5cdf45e8\",\"cmd-fe5e3ecd4c404e05\",\"cmd-1a9afa498dc44815\"]},{\"id\":\"ansible-implementation\",\"requirement\":\"Change implemented using Ansible\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node2 was confirmed as the only node with ansible/ansible-playbook installed (cmd-e384874676f94ccb, cmd-8954a3960f524edd) and used to run a playbook against node1 using ansible.builtin.copy, ansible.builtin.command, and community.general.modprobe, completing with changed=2 (cmd-4b731f48756449b2).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e384874676f94ccb\",\"cmd-8954a3960f524edd\",\"cmd-4b731f48756449b2\"]},{\"id\":\"node1-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on node1 (boot identity before/after, restart ack, recovered state)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id 901ef286... and healthy blacklist state captured (cmd-e44f6c1f1b76433f), reboot acknowledged (cmd-71cc473d5cdf45e8), connection loss observed during boot (cmd-8b14c51e8f164270, cmd-c79d1fbc575d48e3), new boot_id d397e892... confirmed (cmd-fe5e3ecd4c404e05), and recovered/persisted blacklist state verified (cmd-1a9afa498dc44815). Consistent with fixed before_prepare/after_executor boot-identity snapshots for node1 (901ef286... -> d397e892...).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-e44f6c1f1b76433f\",\"cmd-71cc473d5cdf45e8\",\"cmd-8b14c51e8f164270\",\"cmd-fe5e3ecd4c404e05\",\"cmd-1a9afa498dc44815\"]},{\"id\":\"node2-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on node2 (boot identity before/after, restart ack, recovered state)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Pre-restart boot_id 6fd57414... and working ansible install captured (cmd-7ae338b30ee3497e), reboot acknowledged (cmd-7665bd2eaad64ee1), connection loss observed (cmd-dca33bae13774192, cmd-ae56fa5dee2e471c), new boot_id b0b9b35a... confirmed (cmd-5e8358c140e94836), and recovery verified via a successful idempotent playbook re-run against node1 (changed=0) confirming both control tooling and cross-node connectivity recovered (cmd-37770848fe2d49a2). Matches fixed after_executor boot-identity snapshot for node2.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-7ae338b30ee3497e\",\"cmd-7665bd2eaad64ee1\",\"cmd-dca33bae13774192\",\"cmd-5e8358c140e94836\",\"cmd-37770848fe2d49a2\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.65,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands were justified prerequisites: node1's sshd was not running (missing host keys), so ssh-keygen -A, 'systemctl enable --now ssh' (cmd-adf66e128fb04ed7, following the failed attempt cmd-3bdd85671eb74ed0), and writing authorized_keys were necessary to make node1 reachable by Ansible from node2, and the default ansible.cfg's 'antrieb' remote user was correctly worked around with an explicit inventory override (cmd-4b731f48756449b2) rather than modifying shared config. However, this leaves two residual, executor-caused footprint changes beyond the task's scope that were not reverted: (1) sshd on node1 is now a persistently enabled, actively listening service (visible in the after_executor listening-sockets diff, owned by a running sshd process rather than socket-activated systemd as at baseline) with root key-based login enabled — a meaningful and permanent widening of remote access that was not cleaned up after the playbook run completed; (2) cmd-15e2b3c779ab4279 appended the CLUSTER_SSH_PUBKEY to node2's own ~/.ssh/authorized_keys, which has no apparent purpose (node2 is the control node initiating connections, not receiving them) and was left in place. Both were low-risk, bounded, single-purpose mutations rather than broad or destructive ones, so they lower the score moderately rather than severely. No failed/conflicting services, package damage, or abandoned temp files were observed; package-health and failed-services checks were clean before and after.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3bdd85671eb74ed0\",\"cmd-adf66e128fb04ed7\",\"cmd-15e2b3c779ab4279\",\"cmd-4b731f48756449b2\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:listening-sockets\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The core task — blacklisting usb-storage on node1 both immediately and persistently across reboot, implemented via Ansible — is directly and coherently demonstrated: baseline capture, playbook run (changed=2), immediate modprobe-failure verification, a real reboot with boot_id change, and post-reboot re-verification that the block survived. The separate mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was also completed correctly for both node1 and node2, with boot identities matching the fixed lifecycle snapshots. The main issue is operational hygiene: to make Ansible reach node1 at all, the executor had to bootstrap and persistently enable sshd with root key-based login, which remains active afterward and is a genuine (if necessary) widening of node1's attack surface, plus a stray SSH key addition to node2's own authorized_keys with no clear purpose. Neither undermines the task outcome itself.\",\"limitations\":[\"No independent evidence outside the executor's own commands was available to corroborate the reboot timing or the modprobe.d file's exact deployment mechanism beyond the playbook's own reported 'changed' status.\",\"The persistent enabling of root SSH access on node1 is a side effect of environment bootstrapping (missing default 'antrieb' user/sshd) rather than a task requirement, but is noted because it was not reverted.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"immediate-unload","requirement":"usb-storage kernel module is not currently loaded on node1 (module stopped 'now')","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline (cmd-55de29ecc7a0494b) showed the module already unloaded and no blacklist file present. After the playbook ran, an explicit modprobe attempt (cmd-caa57d6719044407) failed with rc=1 and lsmod stayed empty, directly confirming the module is blocked from loading immediately.","evidence_ids":["cmd-55de29ecc7a0494b","cmd-4b731f48756449b2","cmd-2f8d02ea92144dc2","cmd-caa57d6719044407"]},{"id":"persistent-block","requirement":"usb-storage kernel module is prevented from loading after a reboot","status":"satisfied","summary":"A blacklist/install-false modprobe.d config was written and initramfs updated (cmd-4b731f48756449b2). A real reboot was performed (cmd-71cc473d5cdf45e8), boot_id changed from 901ef286... to d397e892... (cmd-e44f6c1f1b76433f, cmd-fe5e3ecd4c404e05), and post-reboot verification (cmd-1a9afa498dc44815) confirmed the config file was intact and modprobe usb-storage still failed (rc=1) with the module unloaded — a direct before/disruption/after demonstration of the persistence requirement.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4b731f48756449b2","cmd-e44f6c1f1b76433f","cmd-71cc473d5cdf45e8","cmd-fe5e3ecd4c404e05","cmd-1a9afa498dc44815"]},{"id":"ansible-implementation","requirement":"Change implemented using Ansible","status":"satisfied","summary":"node2 was confirmed as the only node with ansible/ansible-playbook installed (cmd-e384874676f94ccb, cmd-8954a3960f524edd) and used to run a playbook against node1 using ansible.b